221 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 115 0 R
/E (In recent years, significant research has been carried out in the field of microstrip antenna arrays, especially for applications in MIMO \(Multiple Input Multiple Output\) and Synthetic Aperture Radar \(SAR\) systems. Researchers have focused on improving antenna performance in terms of bandwidth, gain, polarization diversity, and compactness. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
222 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 115 0 R
/E (Traditional microstrip antennas are widely used due to their simplicity and ease of fabrication. However, they suffer from limitations such as narrow bandwidth and low efficiency, which restrict their use in advanced radar systems. To address these issues, various techniques have been proposed.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
223 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 115 0 R
/E (One approach involves the use of antenna arrays, where multiple radiating elements are combined to improve gain and directivity. Array configurations such as linear and planar arrays have been studied extensively to achieve better radiation performance.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
224 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 115 0 R
/E (Another important advancement is the introduction of dual-band antennas, which allow operation at two different frequency ranges. This is particularly useful in SAR systems where multiple frequency bands are required for enhanced imaging and target detection.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
225 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 115 0 R
/E (In addition, dual-polarized antennas have gained attention due to their ability to transmit and receive signals in two orthogonal polarizations. This improves signal diversity, reduces multipath interference, and enhances overall system reliability.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
226 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 115 0 R
/E (To support multiple antenna elements in an array, different feeding techniques have been explored. Among them, the corporate feeding network is widely used because it ensures equal power distribution and better impedance matching across all elements.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
227 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 115 0 R
/E (Recent studies have also focused on compact antenna designs, where the size of the antenna is reduced without significantly affecting performance. This is essential for modern applications where space and weight constraints are critical.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
